  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_ANIMALKINGDOM_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Sectional view of pseudocoelomate body cavity</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The given diagrammatic sectional view illustrates a specific type of body cavity organisation in triploblastic animals. Identify the body cavity labelled <strong>X</strong> and the phylum that possesses this organisation.</p>
      <p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 340 240" width="340" height="240" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="340" height="240" fill="#ffffff"/>
          <!-- Outer Ectoderm -->
          <circle cx="130" cy="120" r="88" fill="#b8b5e8" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"/>
          <!-- Unlined Body Cavity -->
          <circle cx="130" cy="120" r="68" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"/>
          <!-- Scattered Mesodermal Pouches -->
          <ellipse cx="130" cy="72" rx="18" ry="10" fill="#dc2626" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="130" cy="168" rx="18" ry="10" fill="#dc2626" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="82" cy="120" rx="10" ry="18" fill="#dc2626" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="178" cy="120" rx="10" ry="18" fill="#dc2626" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- Inner Endoderm -->
          <circle cx="130" cy="120" r="34" fill="#d1d5db" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"/>
          <!-- Gut Lumen -->
          <circle cx="130" cy="120" r="20" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"/>
          <!-- Labels -->
          <line x1="162" y1="88" x2="235" y2="55" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <circle cx="162" cy="88" r="2.5" fill="#111827"/>
          <text x="242" y="60" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">X (Body cavity)</text>

          <line x1="184" y1="120" x2="235" y2="120" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <circle cx="184" cy="120" r="2.5" fill="#111827"/>
          <text x="242" y="125" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#111827">Mesoderm pouch</text>

          <line x1="152" y1="145" x2="235" y2="180" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <circle cx="152" cy="145" r="2.5" fill="#111827"/>
          <text x="242" y="185" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#111827">Endoderm (Gut)</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The cross-sectional diagram displays an outer ectodermal body wall, an inner endodermal gut wall, four scattered mesodermal pouches, and an intervening body cavity labelled <strong>X</strong> that is not lined by a continuous mesodermal layer.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Sign Conventions</strong><br/>The diagram represents a transverse section of a triploblastic animal, where the nature of the cavity between the body wall and gut wall is determined by whether mesoderm forms a complete lining.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>A true coelom is lined by mesoderm on all sides. When the body cavity is not lined by mesoderm and instead the mesoderm is present as scattered pouches between the ectoderm and endoderm, the cavity is called a pseudocoelom, and animals possessing it are called pseudocoelomates.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>1. In the diagram, the mesoderm is clearly arranged as isolated pouches rather than a continuous lining around the cavity <strong>X</strong>.<br/>2. Therefore, cavity <strong>X</strong> is a pseudocoelom.<br/>3. Among animal phyla, Aschelminthes (roundworms) are pseudocoelomates, whereas Platyhelminthes are acoelomates and Annelida are coelomates.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Both the scattered mesodermal pouches and the unlined cavity uniquely identify the pseudocoelomate condition characteristic of Aschelminthes. Thus, the correct answer is Pseudocoelom; Aschelminthes.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
