  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_ANIMALKINGDOM_DIAGRAM_Q03 - Fundamental characteristics of Phylum Chordata</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The given schematic diagram represents the fundamental characteristics of Phylum Chordata. Which combination correctly identifies the structures labelled <strong>P</strong>, <strong>Q</strong>, <strong>R</strong>, and <strong>S</strong>?</p>
      <p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 420 220" width="420" height="220" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="420" height="220" fill="#ffffff"/>
          <!-- Body outline -->
          <path d="M 35 120 C 35 75, 110 60, 220 75 C 300 85, 360 100, 395 110 C 360 125, 310 132, 290 132 C 250 132, 220 155, 145 155 C 75 155, 35 145, 35 120 Z" fill="#fde68a" stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"/>
          <!-- Dorsal hollow nerve cord (P) -->
          <path d="M 55 100 C 110 82, 220 84, 370 108" fill="none" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <!-- Notochord (Q) -->
          <path d="M 65 112 C 120 96, 220 98, 360 113" fill="none" stroke="#ea580c" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <!-- Pharyngeal gill slits (R) -->
          <ellipse cx="95" cy="130" rx="4" ry="10" fill="#9ca3af" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="110" cy="130" rx="4" ry="10" fill="#9ca3af" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="125" cy="130" rx="4" ry="10" fill="#9ca3af" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="140" cy="130" rx="4" ry="10" fill="#9ca3af" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <!-- Label P -->
          <line x1="115" y1="88" x2="115" y2="38" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="110" y="30" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">P</text>
          <!-- Label Q -->
          <line x1="210" y1="100" x2="210" y2="38" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="205" y="30" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">Q</text>
          <!-- Label R -->
          <line x1="117" y1="140" x2="117" y2="185" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="112" y="202" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">R</text>
          <!-- Bracket and Label S (Post-anal part) -->
          <path d="M 295 142 L 295 152 L 395 152 L 395 142" fill="none" stroke="#111827" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="340" y="172" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#111827">S</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The longitudinal schematic depicts the general body plan of a chordate with four labelled anatomical features: a dorsalmost longitudinal cord <strong>P</strong>, a rod-like structure immediately ventral to it labelled <strong>Q</strong>, a series of vertical openings in the anterior ventral region labelled <strong>R</strong>, and the posterior body extension behind the anus labelled <strong>S</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Assumptions and Sign Conventions</strong><br/>In the standard chordate schematic, the top surface represents the dorsal side, the bottom surface represents the ventral side, the left end represents the anterior (head) region, and the right end represents the posterior (tail) region.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Relevant Formulae and Principles</strong><br/>Phylum Chordata is fundamentally characterised by the presence of a dorsal, hollow, single nerve cord; a mesodermally derived rod-like notochord located ventral to the nerve cord; paired pharyngeal gill slits; and a post-anal tail.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>1. Label <strong>P</strong> points to the dorsalmost longitudinal structure, which is the dorsal hollow nerve cord.<br/>2. Label <strong>Q</strong> points to the supportive rod-like structure positioned just beneath the nerve cord, which is the notochord.<br/>3. Label <strong>R</strong> points to the paired openings perforating the pharynx, which are the gill slits.<br/>4. Label <strong>S</strong> marks the posterior region extending beyond the anal opening, which is the post-anal part (tail).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 5: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Checking the relative dorsal-ventral and anterior-posterior positions confirms all four chordate hallmarks. Thus, the correct identification is P: Nerve cord, Q: Notochord, R: Gill slits, S: Post-anal part.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
